// REJECTED — G-RETAIN: a `Retained[T, P]` value whose retention policy's
// deadline has passed may not reach a PERSISTENCE SINK (roadmap item 472).
//
// `customer_pii` declares the five facts a retention policy carries: the
// deadline (`until`), the geographic residence, who may request deletion
// (`deleters`), which derivative classes the policy covers (`derivatives`), and
// — by its absence here — that no legal hold is in force. `load` hands back a
// value under that policy, so the value carries the `retained:customer_pii`
// origin from the moment it enters the value world.
//
// `db_put` declares `emission[db.insert]`. `db` is a DURABLE-STORAGE scope
// (revl.retention.PERSISTENCE_SINK_SCOPES), so the crossing is a persistence
// sink — read off the capability the crossing DECLARES, never off an author
// qualifier, because the side that owns the store is the side that knows it is
// one. Note that `db_put`'s own parameter carries no qualifier: the refusal
// follows the VALUE, not the spelling at the sink.
//
// The deadline here is in the past absolutely (2020), so this file is refused
// under any clock. A policy whose deadline has not passed compiles, and so does
// this one with `hold: "<matter>"` added: a declared legal hold overrides the
// deadline, which is what a legal hold is for.
//
// Honest scope: this refuses a WRITE the compiler can see. It does not prove
// that data already written was destroyed — that is the erasure receipt's job,
// and a receipt is an enumeration plus a signature, never a proof of
// destruction (revl.retention, docs/design/472-retention-erasure-receipts.md).
//
// expected error:
//   data past its retention deadline may not be written to durable storage

retention customer_pii {
  until: "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"
  residence: "eu"
  deleters: dpo, subject
  derivatives: summary, index
}

extern pure fn load(k: Str) -> Retained[Str, customer_pii] = @py { return k }

extern emission[db.insert] fn db_put(row: Str) -> Int = @py { return 0 }

service Ops { emission fn go(k: Str) -> Int }

component Store provides ops: Ops {
  provide ops {
    fn go(k) {
      let row = load(k)
      let n = db_put(row)
      return n
    }
  }
}
